+/**
+ * @brief How far ahead the player's own body is tested for an obstacle.
+ *
+ * Just over a stride. Long enough to arrive slightly before the player
+ * does, short enough that it is describing what is stopping them rather
+ * than what is across the room.
+ */
+#define ETA_OBSTACLE_PROBE_DISTANCE 48.0f
+
+/**
+ * @brief Says what is blocking the way, and only when something is.
+ *
+ * This used to trace a ray from the eye and announce whatever it hit
+ * within a couple of metres. A tester reported it saying "wall, 0
+ * metres" while they walked on perfectly well, and they were right to
+ * call it noise: a ray from the eye answers "what am I looking at",
+ * which is a different question from "what is stopping me".
+ *
+ * Two things it got wrong, and both were common. Stairs: every step has
+ * an upright face, so a ray sees a wall on every staircase in the game
+ * while the player climbs it without difficulty. And any wall beside a
+ * corridor, which is a wall you are looking at rather than one you are
+ * walking into.
+ *
+ * So the probe is now the player's own bounding box, pushed forward the
+ * way the engine pushes it, with its base lifted by the engine's own
+ * step height. If the box can move, nothing is in the way and nothing is
+ * said - a stair riser passes under it, exactly as the player's feet do.
+ * If the box cannot move, the player cannot either, which is the only
+ * moment this is worth saying.
+ *
+ * The distance goes too. When it only fires against something the player
+ * is up against, the number is always nearly zero, and "0 metres" was
+ * the part that read as broken.
+ */
 static void ETA_NarrateObstacleAhead(void)
 {
 	static char lastDescription[128];
 	static int  lastTime;
 	char        description[128];
-	trace_t     trace;
-	int         distance;
